WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.270
- Alright, we are here in sunny Los Angeles,

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.740
^and we're gonna be doing an episode on pork.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.020
^- [Andrew] So we've done American barbecue pork ribs before.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.190
- Oh yeah, well today we're doing pork Filipino-style.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.053
I am so excited for Filipino food, let's go.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:14.903
Today on Worth It,

00:00:14.903 --> 00:00:16.900
we're gonna be trying three Filipino pork dishes

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.440
at three drastically different price points

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.010
to find out which one is the most worth it at it's price.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:24.180
I have quite the number of Filipino friends telling me

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.570
make a Worth It episode around Filipino food.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.587
- Okay, humble brag.

00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:29.463
- 'Cause I have so many friends?

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:30.553
- Yeah.

00:00:30.553 --> 00:00:31.590
- Oh no, I don't have that many friends.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.450
I just pretend like I do.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.346
I only have two friends and they're in this car.

00:00:35.346 --> 00:00:37.596
(laughing)

00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:40.780
- That's a good moment to introduce the fact

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.245
that we have four people in the car.

00:00:42.245 --> 00:00:43.231
(laughing)

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.333
- [Steven] That's Annie.

00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:45.260
- Cool, so.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.620
- The first place we're going to is called RiceBar.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.450
We're gonna eat with a friend, her name is Allie.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.230
She's Filipino, she loves pork.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.800
- Pork equals pig, is that correct?

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.760
- Porky the Pig, that's right.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.740
Oh man, I just got sad.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.340
I imagined eating Porky the Pig.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.750
^(upbeat music)

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.870
^- I'm Charles Olalia, chef, owner of RiceBar

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.230
^here in downtown Los Angeles.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.290
- What type of establishment is RiceBar?

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.100
- Everyday Filipino fair,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.020
something that you'd only find in homes,

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.410
you know like the holy trinity of breakfast:

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.600
garlic rice, which is essential to the Philippines,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.190
the egg, and a longanisa.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.140
So the pork longanisa, it's essential Filipino sausage.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.240
The one we make is from my region of Pampanga,

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.220
but we've added a little bit of L.A. flair to it already,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.810
so it's a seven ounce sausage,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.770
more geared towards the appetite of how we eat over here.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.370
We start off with fresh ground pork.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.160
We don't ground the fat, we actually dice it.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.623
It gives better texture to the sausage.

00:01:33.623 --> 00:01:37.210
Strain out for two days, stuff it in a natural hog casing,

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.150
and let it cure again for another three days.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.020
The garlic, the pepper, the sugar, the salt

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.750
can really penetrate that meat

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.320
and by the time we serve it to the guest,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.810
the flavors are way more robust.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.790
We cook it slowly on the griddle until it caramelizes

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.420
so you have a beautiful glaze on the sausage.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.020
Then we pair that off in a garlic friend rice,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.850
and we make a very intense garlic oil,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.180
like a one-to-one ration of garlic to oil.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.600
We got our rice toasted and coat raw,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.400
then we put our garlic stock

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.100
in a little bit of annatto water

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.140
to give it the yellow color.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.800
You have all the flavor to the very inside of the grain.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.890
- [Andrew] So it's cooked in that garlic stock?

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.960
- Yes, and then we pair that off with pickled papaya

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.350
which rounds off the whole dish.

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.950
^- [Steven] Wow, I want that one.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:14.573
^- Hello.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:15.703
^- Hi.

00:02:15.703 --> 00:02:16.536
^- This is Allie.

00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:17.369
^So you're Filipino.

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:18.202
^(laughing)

00:02:18.202 --> 00:02:21.080
- Longanisa is something that I used to have growing up,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.590
usually at carnivals and fairs, that kind of thing.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.540
Or you would like have it for breakfast.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.840
- So this brings you back?

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:27.723
- Yeah!

00:02:27.844 --> 00:02:29.460
Have you guys eaten Filipino-style before?

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.060
You have the spoon in the right hand, fork in the left hand,

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.470
and you shovel the food onto the spoon.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:35.310
Do you want to try it?

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.143
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:36.976
- [Steven] Together!

00:02:37.016 --> 00:02:37.849
- [Allie] Okay, ready?

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:38.682
- [All] Cheers.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.521
- It's got that sweetness to it, huh?

00:02:43.521 --> 00:02:44.480
(laughing)

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.960
There's so many things going on with this sausage.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.630
Good texture, and then it's a little spice,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.170
and it's just like everything's nice.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.370
- [Andrew] I love sausage,

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.380
sausage might be my single favorite food.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:55.213
- Why?

00:02:55.213 --> 00:02:56.640
- I grew up eating a lot of sausage.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.600
If you think about what is going on into making a sausage,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.240
it's crazy but also genius.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.120
Repackaging an animal into like

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.163
a delicious little flavor missal.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:07.932
(laughing)

00:03:07.932 --> 00:03:08.980
- It's kinda gross, actually.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.791
- So this is the spicy vinegar that you--

00:03:10.791 --> 00:03:11.624
- Ooh, let's hit that up.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.440
- One of the major flavors

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.440
of the Philippines, this sourness.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.883
And so that spicy vinegar goes well with everything.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.203
- Oh yeah.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:21.403
- Oh!

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:22.393
(laughing)

00:03:22.393 --> 00:03:24.580
- Yeah, sweetness in the sausage with the spicy vinegar,

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.470
that's it right there.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.201
- So this is like a perfect Filipino breakfast.

00:03:28.201 --> 00:03:30.490
Egg, rice, some kind of meat product.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:32.815
- I could get obsessed with the garlic rice.

00:03:32.815 --> 00:03:33.968
- It's so amazing.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:36.000
- Like, that alone, I would travel far and wide for it.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.550
- It's really good to have

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:38.627
something like this in a restaurant,

00:03:38.627 --> 00:03:40.807
because I know growing up I only had it at home

00:03:40.807 --> 00:03:42.760
and I can like take my friends here,

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.070
who have never had this before.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.038
- Are you ready, this is gonna be a big bite.

00:03:46.038 --> 00:03:46.871
(Allie laughing)

00:03:46.871 --> 00:03:47.704
- Whoa!

00:03:47.704 --> 00:03:48.660
^- What do you think?

00:03:48.943 --> 00:03:50.570
Adam just whispered it's really good.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:52.370
The quieter he says it, the more he means it.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.610
- The other dish we're gonna have

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.160
is our house made spam, tocino.

00:03:55.391 --> 00:03:57.410
Tocino in the Philippines is kinda like our bacon.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.090
Again, diced fat, high in sugar, very simple.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.210
We cook 'em in a spam can in a water bath,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.050
let that rest for about three days.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:04.890
Then we slice it off,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.520
brulee it with a little bit of sugar

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.430
so we get the nice caramelization.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.180
We're gonna pair that off with black rice,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.280
an heirloom variety from south of the Philippines,

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.733
very high in anthocyanins,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.470
just like with blueberries and spinach.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.740
^Some pickled radishes, and some choi.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:18.573
- [Steven] Okay!

00:04:18.710 --> 00:04:19.723
- That is beautiful.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:20.963
- [Andrew] Yeah.

00:04:20.963 --> 00:04:22.060
- It's got color, texture,

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.210
God, there's nothing I loved more than spam as a kid.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.043
- [All] Cheers.

00:04:27.028 --> 00:04:27.880
(Steven groans)

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.420
- Hot damn.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.253
- Mhm, oh damn.

00:04:30.253 --> 00:04:31.233
- [Allie] That is so good.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.490
- The rice is like seriously blowing me away.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.563
^Why does it almost taste cocoa-y to me?

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:39.063
- [Steven] Very delicious,

00:04:39.063 --> 00:04:41.160
and with all these other ingredients in there, look at that!

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.710
- [Allie] It looks like a vegetable garden.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.350
- If only spam could grow from the ground.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.183
- On trees.

00:04:45.183 --> 00:04:46.016
- Thank you so much for joining us!

00:04:46.016 --> 00:04:46.849
- You're very welcome!

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:48.543
^- Thank you for having us.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.780
- I think family style is pretty much

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.060
the only way to eat if you're Filipino

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.360
because so much of the food

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.730
is about community and about family.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.703
- Would you even say that we're family?

00:04:58.480 --> 00:04:59.313
- You and I?

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.197
- I mean, the three of us, yeah.

00:05:01.197 --> 00:05:02.030
- Oh.

00:05:02.265 --> 00:05:03.180
(laughing)

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.013
We're something.

00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:05.365
(laughing)

00:05:05.365 --> 00:05:07.100
- RiceBar, what a treat.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.010
Before we go into our pork fact,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:10.510
^we're gonna be hitting up Jollibee

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.170
^for a quick halo-halo break.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:13.360
^- [Andrew] Oh look, there's the bee.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.770
^- [Steven] Bee's got a big bosom.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:16.060
- Do you know what bosom is?

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.110
- Isn't it your butt?

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.323
- No, it's a women's chest area.

00:05:19.928 --> 00:05:22.178
(laughing)

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:24.893
- I did not know that.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.500
(upbeat music)

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.360
- Wild!

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.923
I like this whole cake on top.

00:05:30.453 --> 00:05:31.286
(Steven moans)

00:05:31.286 --> 00:05:32.119
- Pork fact!

00:05:32.119 --> 00:05:34.450
^Only about 30% of pork in consumed fresh,

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:36.130
^the rest is cured or smoked

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.420
^and eaten as bacon, ham, and sausage.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.980
- Bacon, ham, sausage, that's a pretty strong

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:43.390
team of foods right there.

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.980
- Right, they should be holding the ball, playing the games.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.113
- Holding the pigskin.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:47.993
- Boom!

00:05:47.993 --> 00:05:49.310
- We're gonna stop at a place called LASA,

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.033
get some thick cut pork belly!

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:52.883
I've been told that I don't

00:05:52.883 --> 00:05:54.303
^have the voice of a radio person.

00:05:55.784 --> 00:05:57.080
(Steven inhales)

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:57.913
- Roasted.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.660
^(upbeat music)

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.120
^- I'm chef Chad Valencia, co-owner of LASA.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.130
We're a seasonal, Filipino-inspired restaurant

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.220
here in Chinatown, L.A.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:09.440
- From my understanding, lasa in Tagalog is flavor?

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.404
- Flavor or taste.

00:06:10.404 --> 00:06:12.750
- Can you talk about what that means for you guys?

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.250
- It's based on our life experiences,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.590
so this is not traditional Filipino food by any means.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.710
My brother and I come from California restaurants,

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.060
so we follow the seasons.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.600
What's popping at the market is gonna dictate

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.240
what ingredients are gonna get used.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.790
We'll think what can coincide with Filipino dishes

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.760
but isn't that specific ingredient.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.630
For instance, in springtime,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.200
there's a sour soup called sinigang.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.940
We use rhubarb to sour the broth.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.660
That's not traditional by any means,

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.710
but if any Filipino were to taste that broth,

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:38.543
they would know is sinigang.

00:06:38.543 --> 00:06:39.610
- And you're using rhubarb

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.070
'cause that's what you have here in California?

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:41.903
- Exactly.

00:06:41.903 --> 00:06:44.000
We're gonna eat our twice-cooked pork belly.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.010
It's based on a Filipino dish called kalabasa ginataan,

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.980
which means squash stewed in coconut.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:51.060
There's always the addition

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.790
of pork product and shrimp product

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.620
in a lot of vegetable dishes in the Philippines,

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.120
so we've flipped it on it's head

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.950
and turned it into an entree.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:01.200
We take skin-on, boneless pork belly slabs

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.550
and slowly render on a plancha

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.440
so that the skin ends up crispy.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.050
We stew the kabocha squash in coconut milk

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.540
until it's extremely tender,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.373
and then we puree it.

00:07:10.373 --> 00:07:12.100
And then we lock roast Chinese long beans,

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:13.640
and then we take mustard greens

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.210
and we dress that in butter and vinegar.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.030
So we have this fresh salad

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.370
to kinda cut through the richness of the belly

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:21.210
and even the kabocha and coconut.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.090
People are always quick to say

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.000
oh, you guys are doing Filipino-fusion.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.550
Filipino food is already fusion

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.680
by a way of China, Spain, America.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.740
We're not doing anything that different,

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.970
^we're just doing California Filipino food.

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:34.803
^- [Steven] Let's try it.

00:07:34.803 --> 00:07:35.636
^- [Andrew] Yeah, I'm ready to eat.

00:07:35.636 --> 00:07:38.323
^- Green mango soda, cheers.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.573
- Oh, gentle bubbles.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.460
Sodas are better when the bubbles are gentle.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.660
- Agreed, let's eat.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.400
- I'm gonna do a little puree taste-test solo mode

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:49.603
before I get into this.

00:07:49.603 --> 00:07:50.436
- Alright.

00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:51.269
- Cheers.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:52.743
Whoa!

00:07:52.743 --> 00:07:53.710
- It's almost like pumpkin pie.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.810
- At first I was like, okay this is squash.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.590
And then there's this whole other flavors

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.950
that run across your mouth.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:03.120
- Okay so here we got a thick cut pork belly.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:03.953
(laughing)

00:08:03.953 --> 00:08:05.433
it's like a steak of pork!

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:06.890
- [Andrew] Imma do a little squash.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.960
- Yes, I'm going long beans, too.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:09.793
Cheers!

00:08:10.161 --> 00:08:12.578
(calm music)

00:08:12.854 --> 00:08:15.604
(Steven moaning)

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.393
- The hard exterior of this pork belly,

00:08:18.456 --> 00:08:20.100
(tapping)

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.083
very satisfying.

00:08:21.694 --> 00:08:22.920
(food crunching)

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:23.753
And that.

00:08:23.753 --> 00:08:24.733
- [Steven] Crunch!

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:26.930
- How fun is that?

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:27.920
- It's all about into the--

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.957
- The bellies of things, you know?

00:08:29.957 --> 00:08:30.790
- Yeah.

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:31.623
- Of the beast.

00:08:31.623 --> 00:08:32.456
- Of The Rock.

00:08:32.456 --> 00:08:33.289
- [Andrew] The Rock?

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:34.540
- Yeah you know, The Rock like his abs.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:35.720
- Oh, yeah, sure.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.190
- Yeah, he's got a nice belly.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:38.960
We share a lot of dishes on this show.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:39.793
- Uh huh.

00:08:39.793 --> 00:08:42.370
- And we're often times like huddled over one small plate.

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:43.503
- Uh huh.

00:08:43.503 --> 00:08:46.320
- Like nibbling away, this was created to be shared.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.153
- That's right.

00:08:47.153 --> 00:08:49.049
- Big thick cut pork belly.

00:08:49.049 --> 00:08:49.882
(laughing)

00:08:49.882 --> 00:08:51.400
Right in the middle, on a huge plate.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.350
Hey, come over here.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:53.847
Family style.
- Family boy.

00:08:54.824 --> 00:08:56.130
- You wanna double down?

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:57.003
- Double down!

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.810
- Ugh, why'd you do that so slowly?

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.883
Hold out your hand, and I'm gonna fly the camera into it.

00:09:04.993 --> 00:09:07.326
(whooshing)

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:09.433
- We're cool.

00:09:09.443 --> 00:09:10.530
(laughing)

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:11.973
Plot twist!

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.583
We're in New York City, whoo!

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:15.873
And we're here because this

00:09:15.873 --> 00:09:18.130
is a very special dish that we're gonna try.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:20.370
In fact, it's not even a dish, it is a feast.

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.250
But before we get there.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.090
- I know what's happening.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:23.963
- Oh, we all know what's happening.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:24.863
- It's no longer a surprise.

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:25.696
- That guy knows what's happening.

00:09:25.696 --> 00:09:26.529
- Pork fact!

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:27.403
- Ow!

00:09:27.403 --> 00:09:30.950
^Hundreds of years ago, all pork was referred to as bacoun,

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:32.950
^or as it was spelled back then, bacoun?

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:35.550
B-A-C-O-U-N.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:36.630
- Yeah, you have to spell it!

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.790
^- It comes from the old teutonic, backe,

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.670
^that referred to the back of the hog.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:42.980
^- Bacon, bacoun.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:44.400
- How's that for a fact?

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:45.713
- Borderline factual.

00:09:45.857 --> 00:09:47.270
(laughs)

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:48.410
Pretty good, it's pretty good.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.072
- Pretty good, thank you, thank you very much.

00:09:50.072 --> 00:09:51.850
We're going to a restaurant called Jeepney.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:53.970
They serve what's called a kamayan feast.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:58.913
In the middle of it, we're gonna have a whole pig, stuffed.

00:09:59.817 --> 00:10:00.650
- Ooh.

00:10:01.248 --> 00:10:03.998
(tropical music)

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.320
^- My name's Nicole Ponseca,

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:08.130
^and we're at Jeepney here in the East Village.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.970
You're gonna eat Nueva York style lechon,

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.720
and we're also gonna do a kamayan feast.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.070
- [Steven] What is kamayan?

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.280
- Kamayan is basically to eat with your hands.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.660
We lay out all the food on banana leaves very beautifully,

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.880
because we know that we eat with our eyes first.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.190
But it wasn't really like that for me growing up.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.520
Kamayan was just my dad in the kitchen,

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.100
eating on a plate just with our hands,

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.820
like really simple, really humble.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.890
My whole mission is how can I help spread Filipino food,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.350
but also how can I take anything

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.910
that I was ever embarrassed or ashamed about

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.650
and make it into something that I'm really proud about.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:43.700
So we decided to do it this beautiful way

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.000
of introducing Filipino food in culture,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.210
and Chef Miguel's gonna tell you

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:48.580
about the dishes that we're having today.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:49.910
^- For this particular kamayan,

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:51.800
^we created a special feast for you guys.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.030
We have some dinuguan, some banana ribs,

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:56.840
dampa fry, which is our marinated whole red snapper.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.430
Let's not forget about the chicken adobo,

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:00.270
considered the national dish of the Philippines .

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:03.360
Then you have the star of the show, our whole lechon.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.537
- [Steven] What is lechon, can you kinda walk us into that?

00:11:05.537 --> 00:11:07.370
- [Miguel] Lechon is a whole pig.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.070
Traditionally, we would be roasting it

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:10.500
over an open fire pit.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:11.790
We are in the East Village,

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:13.620
we would probably get our asses busted

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:14.650
for doing something like that.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.050
So we cook our lechon in the oven.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:19.580
We're gonna stuff it with lemongrass, onion, salt, pepper,

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.800
and a shit load of love and longanisa,

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.330
bring that temperature up to 500

00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:25.550
just to crisp that skin and sear everything,

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.290
so we want to trap all those juices in there,

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:29.390
then we lower that temperature and let it roast slowly

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:32.363
so that when you guys get it and crack open that skin,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.100
that meat is just falling off the bone.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.580
The pig that you guys will be having today

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:38.263
is about 22 pounds, so.

00:11:38.329 --> 00:11:39.480
(laughing)

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.490
You guys better be hungry.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:42.410
- We are extremely hungry.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:43.980
Also we're bringing in backup,

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:45.900
what 10 friends, you think that's enough people?

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:47.380
- [Miguel] I think 10 friends is a good number.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:50.423
- Thank you all to our friends for joining us today.

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.920
I just wanna raise a toast to all y'all for coming out.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.753
Cheers guy!

00:11:53.829 --> 00:11:55.079
- [All] Cheers!

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.730
- Oh, we have some hot towels coming.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:00.313
(all cheering)

00:12:00.427 --> 00:12:01.260
Okay!

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.460
^- A common phrase we use for cheers is tagay,

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.036
T-A-G-A-Y, tagay.

00:12:07.036 --> 00:12:08.203
- [All] Tagay!

00:12:08.675 --> 00:12:11.342
(calming music)

00:12:13.657 --> 00:12:15.495
(laughing)

00:12:15.495 --> 00:12:17.078
- Yeah, feeling it?

00:12:17.697 --> 00:12:18.870
- I'm ready to eat with my hands.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:21.010
- I'm gonna teach you all how to do kamayan.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:22.910
You make a little rice ball,

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.250
keep it here in the front fingers.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.080
- And then you push it into your mouth with you thumb.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:27.913
- [Nicole] Yeah.

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:28.746
- Oh!

00:12:28.746 --> 00:12:30.394
- My aunt used to eat like this all the time,

00:12:30.394 --> 00:12:33.068
but I would refuse because I was a bratty little kid.

00:12:33.068 --> 00:12:33.901
(laughing)

00:12:33.901 --> 00:12:35.260
But now, I wish I had paid attention

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.689
because I don't know what I'm doing!

00:12:36.689 --> 00:12:37.939
- [All] Cheers!

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.680
- It's a good sausage.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:42.720
- That tastes like home.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.313
- Do you want to cut into the lechon, is that cool?

00:12:45.489 --> 00:12:47.320
(shouting and laughing)

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.153
Sorry!

00:12:48.153 --> 00:12:48.986
- Nah, that's my fault.

00:12:48.986 --> 00:12:49.819
I'm wanna hand this to Andrew,

00:12:49.819 --> 00:12:51.890
he's usually the one who handles that.

00:12:52.132 --> 00:12:53.465
- [Nicole] Yeah.

00:12:55.189 --> 00:12:56.106
- [All] Oh!

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.830
- [Steven] Look at that steam popping out!

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:01.230
- There's sausages in there.

00:13:07.805 --> 00:13:09.103
- Oh my gosh!

00:13:12.808 --> 00:13:15.391
(all chatting)

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:23.440
Why's it so important, the eating with your hands aspect?

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.540
- It's a way of respecting what you're eating,

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.747
and also interacting with other people at the table

00:13:27.747 --> 00:13:30.420
to catch up on what's going on in your life, you know,

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:32.820
this very intimate moment, vulnerable moment

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:33.690
of eating with your hands.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:35.690
- I believe Brenda wanted some ear.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.390
Do a little quick taste test here.

00:13:37.911 --> 00:13:38.828
- [All] Oh!

00:13:39.937 --> 00:13:41.940
- [Man] Brenda, you monster!

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.263
- Not as strong, oh!

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.240
That's more than just ear.

00:13:45.372 --> 00:13:46.205
- [Woman] Andrew.

00:13:46.205 --> 00:13:47.230
- It's like a fortune cookie.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.035
(food crunching)

00:13:49.035 --> 00:13:51.785
(Andrew moaning)

00:13:53.186 --> 00:13:55.540
- It tastes like a fine bag of that pork you bought.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:56.490
- [Steven] Mm, mhm!

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.310
- The cartilage is almost like glass.

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:01.003
It's like rock candy.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:01.893
- Exactly.

00:14:01.893 --> 00:14:02.726
- Yeah.

00:14:02.726 --> 00:14:03.559
- A salty, delicious.

00:14:03.559 --> 00:14:04.392
- It's so intense!

00:14:04.392 --> 00:14:06.657
- Like you're eating the wisdom of the universe right now.

00:14:06.657 --> 00:14:09.311
You would never know the kind of gossip this pig hears.

00:14:09.311 --> 00:14:11.490
(all laughing)

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.183
- There's a lot happening on this table.

00:14:13.183 --> 00:14:14.016
- (bleep) yeah.

00:14:14.016 --> 00:14:15.980
- Do you think that there is a single bite

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:18.100
that you personally find the most delicious?

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.180
- Yeah, for me (laughing)

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:22.460
I'm gonna go straight for the cheek on that pig.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:23.453
- [Steven] Oh man.

00:14:23.653 --> 00:14:24.570
- [All] Oh!

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:27.650
^- [Andrew] It's so fatty.

00:14:29.488 --> 00:14:31.738
(laughing)

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:34.130
Here, I'll let you have that.

00:14:34.396 --> 00:14:36.548
(tropical music)

00:14:36.548 --> 00:14:39.090
- It's like eating a, you know, melted butter.

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:40.380
That's what it feels like.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.293
- The cheek meat is insane.

00:14:42.590 --> 00:14:43.810
- Alright, give me some.

00:14:43.857 --> 00:14:46.440
(all shouting)

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:51.293
- Wow.

00:14:51.429 --> 00:14:52.262
- That's delicious!

00:14:52.262 --> 00:14:53.095
(laughing)

00:14:53.095 --> 00:14:54.056
It's so fatty!

00:14:54.056 --> 00:14:54.889
- [Andrew] Wait, I don't know where it went.

00:14:54.889 --> 00:14:55.722
I just swallowed it.

00:14:55.722 --> 00:14:57.060
- [Miguel] Second dish, adobo dilaw.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.120
First we take the chicken

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.920
and we poach it for about three minutes,

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.320
just to tighten that skin so when we pan sear it,

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:04.020
that skin isn't falling apart or breaking.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:07.130
We put it in the pot with garlic, and bay leaf,

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:08.950
vinegar, soy, and tumeric,

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:10.900
and let that cook until it's nice and tender.

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:12.890
Take that later, and we're gonna finish it off

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:14.390
by putting it under salamander,

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:16.080
getting that skin nice and crispy.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.420
And we'll finish it off with the sauce.

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:20.270
- [Andrew] Okay, so I think everybody should try the adobo.

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:22.613
- The national dish of the Philippines.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.710
(upbeat music)

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:25.543
- Chicken cheers.

00:15:25.543 --> 00:15:26.376
- Chicken cheers!

00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:27.600
- That part's good.

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:30.772
(Steven groaning)

00:15:31.079 --> 00:15:31.929
- That's so soft!

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.500
- This is the best chicken I've ever had, honestly.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:39.963
- Oh my God, the parade of meats is insane in this dinner.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:44.260
- So the other hidden gem of this meal is the dinuguan.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:45.350
- [Miguel] It's a pork blood stew,

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:46.360
one of my favorite dishes.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:48.710
We take pork butt, which we marinate

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:51.440
in salt, pepper, garlic, and vinegar.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:52.990
It's gonna permeate through the meat

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:54.380
and it's gonna make it more flavorful.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.920
We let it sit overnight, sear it off in a cast iron,

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:58.300
place that in our brazing pan,

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:00.170
take some aromatics, onions and garlic,

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:01.480
along with pork broth.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.930
Cook until it's nice and soft,

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.130
and then we'll be adding the blood once it comes out.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:07.450
It thickens, it becomes this rich, chocolate color,

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.630
and that's ready for serving.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.490
- It's right in front of you, if you see it right here.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:11.473
It's blood stew.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:12.613
Cheers guys.

00:16:12.613 --> 00:16:13.763
- [Andrew And Allie] Cheers.

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:17.160
- [Andrew] Oh man.

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.620
- It's almost smokey, it's very rich.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:20.880
- It's almost like minerally,

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.830
so even though it's likes really rich and deep,

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.670
the minerality kind of cuts it a little bit.

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:27.860
- It's crazy the range of pork flavors that you get here.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.657
Sweet longanisa and the really fatty lechon,

00:16:31.297 --> 00:16:34.360
but you also have this really deep stew.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:36.480
- I just wanna say thank you everybody

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:38.222
for joining us for this awesome meal.

00:16:38.222 --> 00:16:40.472
(cheering)

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:42.610
Is there anything else you wanna say

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:44.753
past the Filipino food?

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:48.223
- (foreign language) Just, let's eat.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.623
- Let's eat, cheers guys.

00:16:51.101 --> 00:16:51.934
- [All] Cheers!

00:16:51.934 --> 00:16:52.767
- Goodnight!

00:16:54.985 --> 00:16:57.652
(chanting Adam)

00:16:59.546 --> 00:17:02.129
(upbeat music)

00:17:06.825 --> 00:17:07.870
- [Allie] Yeah?

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:08.920
^- Pretty good, right?

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:11.533
^Cool.

00:17:11.533 --> 00:17:13.620
- This is hard, but I'm gonna ask you,

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:14.530
and I expect an answer

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:16.040
because that's what we do on this show.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:17.670
Which Filipino pork dish

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:19.160
was the most worth it to you at it's price?

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:23.000
- Every place was the perfect thing in it's category.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.420
LASA would be my perfect date-night meal.

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:29.100
RiceBar is the ultimate fill-you-up-good snack.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:32.070
And Jeepney is just the perfect celebration meal.

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.500
Choosing between them is just like--

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:34.333
- I could tell you mine.

00:17:34.333 --> 00:17:35.316
- What kind of day are you gonna, okay,

00:17:35.316 --> 00:17:36.361
^- [Steven] RiceBar.

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:37.303
^- [Andrew] Oh (beep).

00:17:37.303 --> 00:17:38.610
^- I loved that restaurant feeling

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.377
^that you could be family with strangers there.

00:17:40.377 --> 00:17:43.123
Ugh, it's so hard, but that's, yeah, yup.

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:44.023
- Yeah.

00:17:44.023 --> 00:17:44.856
- Your turn!

00:17:44.856 --> 00:17:47.070
^- I think Jeepney's slightly edges it out for me.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.010
^The format of the meal is so fun

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:51.490
^that you're guaranteed a memorable meal.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.050
Something about eating with your hands,

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:54.370
shoulder to shoulder with people.

00:17:54.370 --> 00:17:56.423
- This is hard, this hurts.

00:17:57.745 --> 00:18:00.473
LASA, no way-sa!
- Adam chooses LASA.

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:01.653
- Three way split.

00:18:01.653 --> 00:18:04.480
- I think I have a crush on Filipino cuisine.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:09.040
It's stuck in my mind, sweet meat with a vinegar touch,

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:10.340
like I just want more.

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:11.470
- [Steven] You wake up thinking about it.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:13.640
- [Andrew] You go to sleep tasting it in your belches.

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:14.953
- [Steven] Oh yes!

